            <ArticleTitle>Non-linear relationship between maternal work hours and child body weight: Evidence from the Western Australian Pregnancy Cohort (Raine) Study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>52-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0277-9536(17)30347-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.socscimed.2017.05.046</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Using longitudinal data from the Western Australia Pregnancy Cohort (Raine) Study and both random-effects and fixed-effects models, this study examined the connection between maternal work hours and child overweight or obesity. Following children in two-parent families from early childhood to early adolescence, multivariate analyses revealed a non-linear and developmentally dynamic relationship. Among preschool children (ages 2 to 5), we found lower likelihood of child overweight and obesity when mothers worked 24 h or less per week, compared to when mothers worked 35 or more hours. This effect was stronger in low-to-medium income families. For older children (ages 8 to 14), compared to working 35-40 h a week, working shorter hours (1-24, 25-34) or longer hours (41 or more) was both associated with increases in child overweight and obesity. These non-linear effects were more pronounced in low-to-medium income families, particularly when fathers also worked long hours.</AbstractText>
